.TH MULTICAP 1 "January 2026" "multicap 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
multicap \- high-performance multicast stream capture utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B multicap
.B \-\-multi
.I address
.B \-i
.I interface
.B \-\-port
.I port
.RB { \-\-pcap | \-\-raw }
.I file
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B multicap
captures multicast UDP streams and writes them to either PCAP files (for analysis with Wireshark, tcpdump, etc.) or raw payload files (e.g., MPEG-TS streams).
.PP
Designed for high-bitrate streams (>1.5 Gbps), it uses a threaded architecture with a large ring buffer to decouple network I/O from disk I/O, minimizing packet loss.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Required Options
.TP
.BR \-\-multi ", " \-m " " \fIaddress\fR
Multicast group address to join (e.g., 239.1.1.1). Must be a valid multicast address in the range 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255.
.TP
.BR \-i " " \fIinterface\fR
Network interface to capture on (e.g., eth0, ens18).
.TP
.BR \-\-port ", " \-p " " \fIport\fR
UDP port number to listen on.
.SS Output Options (one required)
.TP
.BR \-\-pcap " " \fIfile\fR
Write packets to a PCAP file. The file will contain reconstructed IP/UDP headers suitable for analysis with packet capture tools. Use
.B \-
to write to standard output.
.TP
.BR \-\-raw " " \fIfile\fR
Write raw payload data to a file. RTP headers are automatically stripped if present, making this suitable for capturing MPEG-TS or other payload formats. Use
.B \-
to write to standard output.
.SS Optional
.TP
.BR \-\-duration ", " \-d " " \fIseconds\fR
Capture duration in seconds. Default is 0 (unlimited, until interrupted).
.TP
.BR \-\-buffer ", " \-b " " \fImegabytes\fR
Ring buffer size in megabytes. By default, automatically sizes to 25% of free memory (minimum 64 MB). If specified, the requested size will be used but capped at 25% of free memory.
.TP
.BR \-\-quiet ", " \-q
Suppress statistics output. Automatically enabled when writing to standard output.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Display help message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, no packets dropped.
.TP
.B 1
Error (invalid arguments, socket error, file error, etc.).
.TP
.B 2
Success, but some packets were dropped due to buffer overflow.
.SH EXAMPLES
Capture a multicast stream to a PCAP file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
multicap \-\-multi 239.1.1.1 \-i eth0 \-\-port 5000 \-\-pcap capture.pcap
.fi
.RE
.PP
Capture for 60 seconds:
.PP
.RS
.nf
multicap \-\-multi 239.1.1.1 \-i eth0 \-\-port 5000 \-\-pcap capture.pcap \-d 60
.fi
.RE
.PP
Capture raw MPEG-TS stream:
.PP
.RS
.nf
multicap \-\-multi 239.1.1.1 \-i eth0 \-\-port 5000 \-\-raw stream.ts
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipe to tcpdump for live analysis:
.PP
.RS
.nf
multicap \-\-multi 239.1.1.1 \-i eth0 \-\-port 5000 \-\-pcap \- | tcpdump \-r \- \-nn
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipe raw stream to ffprobe:
.PP
.RS
.nf
multicap \-\-multi 239.1.1.1 \-i eth0 \-\-port 5000 \-\-raw \- | ffprobe \-i \-
.fi
.RE
.SH ARCHITECTURE
.B multicap
uses three threads:
.TP
.B Capture thread
Receives packets from the network socket at high priority and pushes them to a lock-free ring buffer.
.TP
.B Writer thread
Pops packets from the ring buffer and writes them to the output file with buffered I/O.
.TP
.B Statistics thread
Displays live capture statistics to standard error.
.PP
The ring buffer is allocated using
.BR mmap (2)
and locked in memory with
.BR mlock (2)
to prevent swapping and ensure consistent performance.
.SH PERFORMANCE TUNING
For optimal performance with high-bitrate streams:
.SS System Socket Buffers
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo sysctl \-w net.core.rmem_max=536870912
sudo sysctl \-w net.core.rmem_default=67108864
.fi
.RE
.SS Network Interface Ring Buffer
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo ethtool \-G eth0 rx 4096
.fi
.RE
.SS CPU Affinity
.PP
.RS
.nf
taskset \-c 2,3 multicap \-\-multi 239.1.1.1 \-i eth0 \-\-port 5000 \-\-pcap out.pcap
.fi
.RE
.SS Capabilities (instead of running as root)
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo setcap cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin,cap_sys_nice+ep /usr/local/bin/multicap
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
Output files are created at the path specified by
.B \-\-pcap
or
.BR \-\-raw .
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Root privileges or appropriate capabilities are required to join multicast groups and bind to network interfaces.
.IP \(bu 2
The PCAP files use linktype RAW (101), containing IP headers without Ethernet framing.
.IP \(bu 2
In raw mode, RTP headers (RFC 3550) are automatically detected and stripped. Non-RTP payloads are written as-is.
.IP \(bu 2
Statistics are written to standard error and are automatically suppressed when output is directed to standard output.
